  "text_md": "## Cardinalities in finite monoids of G-equivariant functions\n\nRamon H. Ruiz-Medina ´* Centro Universitario de Ciencias Exactas e Ingenier´ıas, Universidad de Guadalajara, Guadalajara, Mexico. ´\n\n**Abstract**\n\nA set with a group action is referred to as a *G*-set, and the set of functions that commute with this action forms a monoid under function composition. This paper examines the case where the *G*-set is finite, which implies that the monoid of *G*- equivariant functions is also finite. The document provides formulas for calculating the cardinality of this monoid, its group of units, and explores special cases of *G*- equivariant functions, known as fixing elementary collapsings. All of these results are expressed in terms of specific properties of the *G*-set, including the number of orbits and certain indices of the subgroups acting as stabilizers. **Keywords:** Group actions, *G*-sets, *G*-equivariant function, cardinality.\n\n**MSC 2020:** 20B25, 20E22, 20M20.\n\n### 1 Introduction\n\nFor any group *G*, a *G*-set is simply a set *X* on which *G* acts; that is, there exists a function *·* : *G×X → X* such that *e · x* = *x* for all *x ∈ X* and *g ·* (*h · x*)=(*gh*) *· x* for all *x ∈ X*, *g,h ∈ G* In the context of semigroup theory, *G*-sets are also known as *G*-acts. A *G*-equivariant transformation of *X*, or a *G*-endomorphism of *X*, is a function *τ* : *X→X* such that *τ* (*g · x*) = *g · τ*(*x*)for all *g ∈ G*, *x ∈ X*. These maps are fundamental in the cate- gory of *G*-sets and find applications in various branches of mathematics such as equivariant topology, representation theory, and statistical inference.\n\n# arXiv:2503.03772v1 [math.GR] 4 Mar 2025\n\nThe set of all *G*-equivariant transformations of *X*, which are functions that commute with the group action, forms a monoid under function composition. We denote this monoid as End*G*(*X*), and its group of units, consisting of all bijective *G*-equivariant transforma- tions, as Aut*G*(*X*). These objects have been extensively studied in various contexts (see [2], [3], [10], [16]). Many examples of objects with group actions and associated *G*-equivariant functions have been explored, such as cellular automata, which have been the motivation * Email: harath.ruiz@academicos.udg.mx\n",
